<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sebastián Lerdo de Tejada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1823–1889</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sebastián Lerdo de Tejada was a Mexican liberal lawyer and statesman who served as president from 1872 to 1876. A close minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a> during the war against the French intervention, he succeeded him on his death and wrote the Reform laws into the constitution in 1873. His attempt to secure re-election provoked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz">Porfirio Díaz</a>'s rebellion under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-tuxtepec">Plan of Tuxtepec</a>, and he died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sebastian-lerdo-de-tejada.jpg" alt="Sebastián Lerdo de Tejada" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco de Paula Mendoza · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Presente_Sebasti%C3%A1n_Lerdo_de_Tejada.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
